shared domain and no template_chosen_by_values, so the printed verb can disagree with the printed sign, and the step value is the result of the problem's real operation, so one of the two is always wrong for the item. F06: the verb slot (adding or subtracting) is an independent domain with no tie to the item's operation, so a hint can name the other operation from the item it sits on. F23 (correct_answer and distractors): the numer slot has a second domain that repeats the numerator, which the packet derives from the first operand a_num, not from the result; a keyed answer can therefore print the operand numerator instead of the result numerator. F24 (correct_answer and distractors): the unshaded slot is an arithmetic difference of result_den and result_num, which is the count of unshaded parts; nothing in this lc's families asks for unshaded parts (the questions are F12 and F19, both asking for the result), so as a keyed answer it is wrong. The shared template string {numer}/{denom} also appears on F16, which is a distractor-only use and is not flagged on its own. F04, F05 and F20 were checked and are not failing: F04 and F05 are hints that show the result, which is a hint-design question, not a Rule 1 defect; F20 is tied to operation through template_chosen_by_values and its denominators are constrained by the declared similar_proper bound.\"}",
